[Music]  welcome to the google cloud marketplace  private catalog the private catalog is a  service on google cloud allowing cloud  admins to control distribution of  solutions ensure compliance and  governance and make solutions easily  discoverable within their organization  so what does that really mean an admin  at a company wants to ensure that the  platform's group has access to a set of  cloud solutions some solutions are  custom to the company and others are  solutions they've purchased on google  cloud and they need to be curated by  region by team and different criteria  your organization needs  the way they do this is creating a  catalog instance add solutions to it and  share it with the group of users not  only is it shared private catalog can  ensure that the solution is configured  and ready to deploy or use all in one  place  cloud admins can manage a central place  for end users to find approved solutions  admins have the ability to push  solutions with the latest patches or  versions end users don't have to worry  about what's being deployed and can be  assured that they're always using the  latest and greatest version of every  approved solution  admins can use templates to add  parameters for users they can place  limits on who can deploy resources and  which resources they can deploy  and finally we've made it easy for  admins to take third party solutions  from google cloud marketplace and add  them to their private catalog  we're going to explore the admin view  first which right now is in the zero  state with no catalogs or solutions we  can get started on changing that by  clicking go to catalog manager or manage  solutions on the left side navigation  bar  let's go ahead and create a new solution  start by clicking solutions in the left  navigation bar and then create solution  the private catalog supports three  solution types terraform configs links  and deployment manager  terraform is a tool for building  changing and versioning cloud  infrastructure safely and efficiently  terraform configs are a way to deploy  first party google cloud services but  using terraform  link solutions are really just a url  this could be to documentation or  whatever it is that you need to point to  links work for sas solutions such as a  pointer to mongodb's product offering on  google cloud marketplace  deployment manager solutions are used to  deploy any google cloud first party  service including storage vms containers  or bigquery  for this example we'll select terraform  config  a form will appear on the right side of  the screen the top six fields are  consistent across all solution types  starting with name and description and  also including a tagline for the browse  page of the private catalog an icon  support information and a link to any  relevant documentation  after that we need to add a link to the  terraform config this one is called  google cloud storage and it points to a  zip file in a gcs bucket gs terraform  samples gcsbucket.zip  the zip file should include your  terraform configs  once we've attached the zip file hit  create and this adds the solution to the  private catalog  a process that takes a couple seconds if  you're wondering what's happening in the  back end the system is leveraging cloud  build to run terraform validate and  parse out the input definitions for the  product  while that's happening the solution will  appear in the table and you can click on  it to review the information that was  added earlier  validation status will remain as pending  while the solution is being processed  after which it should change to valid  let's add a send grid email api link  solution  again click solutions in the left  navigation bar  followed by create solution in the bar  above the table  you can also add a deployment manager  solution click create solution in the  bar above the table  enter basic metadata for the solution  and attach a zip file for the solution  this wordpress deployment manager  template was downloaded from the  wordpress solution page available in the  marketplace  now let's create a catalog  first head to catalogs using the left  navigation bar a catalog is really just  a grouping of products and the way to  make your products available to end  users is by creating a catalog adding  solutions to it and then sharing that  catalog with a part of your  organization's google cloud hierarchy  when creating catalogs you may want to  group solutions by region by disciplines  like development or testing or really  whatever makes sense for your company  you can create as many catalogs as you  need  hit the create catalog button the form  prompts us to add a catalog id and a  description this catalog is called  devtools with the descriptor development  tools  click create to finish setting up the  catalog  the new catalog appears in the catalogs  table  now that we've created a catalog we can  assign it a solution click into the  catalog we've just created  in the details page click on assign  solutions  find the google cloud storage solution  we created earlier and press assign  let's go back to the catalogs page using  the back button at the top and add one  more catalog so we can see how they work  together we can name this one test tools  with the descriptor testing tools  once the testing catalog is saved we can  assign the link solution to it  now that we have two catalogs each  containing solutions it's time to share  them with the right users in the google  cloud hierarchy  head back to catalogs page and select  the two catalogs we've created go to  share from the options at the top and  from here you can choose to share the  catalog at the organization folder or  project level we're going to share both  catalogs at the project level with a  project called product manager  everyone in the google cloud hierarchy  with access to that project will now see  and have access to the solutions within  the catalog  we are now going to click browse catalog  on the top right side of the screen to  take a look at the end user view  let's go ahead and filter by the catalog  we created earlier devtools  this brings up our terraform config  google cloud storage clicking on the  solution loads the details page a page  that is similar across all three types  of solutions in the private catalog  this makes it easy for end users to  quickly find solutions they can use  now let's deploy our terraform config  click deploy to begin the new deployment  here we will fill out the input  variables needed for the deployment  for terraform configs we also have  something like a pre-flight check when  we click preview and deploy the system  will start to run terraform plan in the  background this happens without having  to install terraform get the binaries or  anything else these services are  abstracted from you and the end user  allowing you to easily deploy the  solutions you need  in this case terraform plan has run  successfully so we can go ahead and  deploy the solution  in this case we will create a gcs bucket  once the deployment has successfully  completed the icon turns green this  usually takes a few minutes but it does  depend on the solution you are deploying  let's take a look at a situation where  the deployment has failed clicking the  deployment shows the logs enabling the  user to potentially self-service and fix  the issue  with our solution successfully deployed  we can go back to the private catalog  home page and browse all terraform  deployments using the tab in the left  navigation bar  the private catalog gives organizations  a single place to discover approved  solutions that they can deploy knowing  the solutions have been approved and  verified by their cloud admin  it also looks a lot like the google  cloud marketplace and for a reason we've  aimed to create a consistent ui  experience to help you and your users  easily navigate and enjoy the benefits  of the private catalog  ready to get going begin building on  google cloud with 300 in free credits  and 20 plus always free products get  started today

